
:: day_3_1
<<removeobjective "day_2">>
I woke up feeling wrong. Not pain-wrong. That was the strange part. I'd gone to sleep with twelve stitches in my calf and an ankle that looked like someone had inflated it with a bellows, and both of those things should have been screaming at me the moment consciousness arrived. But the pain was muted. Distant. Like hearing thunder from the far side of a mountain range   present but not threatening, a background hum rather than the foreground roar I'd been bracing for.

I lay still for a moment, staring at the ceiling. The water stain shaped like a horse was still there. The cracks in the plaster were still there. Morning light filtered through the shutters in thin golden lines, painting the wall in stripes the color of watered honey. Normal. Familiar. The same ceiling I'd memorized over a week of sleeping in this room.

Everything was the same. Except me.

My skin felt different. Not damaged, not injured   recalibrated. The blanket against my arms was sharper than yesterday. Not rougher   sharper. Like someone had turned up the resolution on every nerve ending in my body. The coarse wool weave that I'd been sleeping under for days without noticing was suddenly a landscape of individual fibers, each one pressing against my skin with its own distinct texture and temperature. The linen of my undershirt against my chest was a whisper where it used to be silence   fabric I'd never been aware of was now making itself known with every breath.

Colors seemed more vivid. The morning light was the same thin autumn gold it had been every day this week, but today it looked richer. Deeper. Like the difference between watered ale and the real thing. The dark wood of the ceiling beams was more detailed, the grain patterns more visible, the knots and whorls more defined.

Mira was asleep beside me. Curled on her side, her back to me, the line of her spine visible through her linen undershirt. The sharp angle of her shoulder blade pressing against the thin fabric. Her cropped copper hair was flat on one side, sticking up at angles on the other, and her breathing was slow and deep and steady. She'd carried me through a goblin mine, across a forest, and through the streets of Valdmere's worst district last night. She'd earned this sleep ten times over.

I sat up. Swung my legs out of bed   carefully, bracing for the ankle to punish me. 

It hurt. But manageable. The poultice Ysolde had packed around it had dried overnight into a stiff, plaster-like shell that actually supported the joint, compressing the swelling and stabilizing the ligament. The calf wound throbbed under its linen bandages, but the angry heat of incipient infection   that specific, sickly warmth that meant rotten wood was poisoning your blood   was gone. Whatever was in that green vial, it was doing its job.

I stood. Tested the weight. The ankle complained but held. Good enough. I hobbled to the mirror.

The small polished tin on the wall   cheap, warped, the kind of mirror that showed you an approximation of your face rather than the real thing. I leaned in. Examined myself in the gray morning light.

Square jaw. Present. Three days of stubble. Present   though was it thinner? I tilted my head. The growth along my upper jaw seemed sparser than it should be. I'd skipped shaving for three days. By now, the shadow should be thick and dark, heading toward a proper beard. Instead, it was... patchy. Light in places. As if some of the follicles had decided to take the morning off.

Green eyes. Present. Deep-set, hooded lids, the same intense stare that had been looking back at me from reflective surfaces since I was old enough to care about reflective surfaces.

The scar through my left eyebrow. Present. The thin diagonal line from a knife fight at twenty-one   the one scar I wore without a story worth telling, because the story was "I was drunk and the other man was drunker and neither of us could fight properly."

Everything where it should be. Everything accounted for. I touched my cheek. My fingers pressed against the skin and    Softer.

Not dramatically. Not the kind of change you'd notice unless you were touching your own face with the specific attention of someone looking for something wrong. But softer. The rough, weathered quality of ten years of outdoor living   sun and wind and road dust baked into the texture of the skin   was diminished. Smoother. Like the top layer of callused skin had been peeled back to reveal something newer and more tender underneath.

I pressed harder. Same bone. Same jaw. Same architecture. The structure was identical. It was the surface that had changed   or was I imagining it?

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Imagination. Exhaustion. That vial.
<</dialog>>

I said it out loud, to the mirror, to the empty room, to whatever part of my brain was raising alarms. After-effects. Ysolde's compound was a powerful healing agent   that much was obvious from the speed of the wound recovery. Heightened sensitivity was a known side effect of strong alchemical treatments. The smoother skin was probably the same mechanism   whatever was accelerating the healing in my calf was also doing something to my surface tissue. Logical. Rational. Nothing to worry about.

I turned away from the mirror. Got dressed quietly. Trousers   they fit the same. Tunic   it fit the same. Belt   buckled at the usual notch. Boots   the right one went on with a wince as it pressed against the swollen ankle, but it went on.
<<addobjective "day_3" "Deal with Grent and the Consortium">>
[[Go downstairs|day_3_2_a]]
[[Wait till She wakes...|day_3_2_b]]

:: day_3_2_b
<<changestat "mira"  2>>
Everything the same. Almost. Behind me, Mira stirred. Mira woke the way she always woke   not gradually, not with stretching and yawning and the slow return of consciousness that most humans experienced. She went from asleep to alert in the space of a single breath, her amber eyes snapping open with the instant focus of a predator who'd spent her entire life knowing that the difference between waking up fast and waking up slow was sometimes the difference between waking up at all.

She stretched. Winced. Whatever carrying a full-grown man through a forest had done to her shoulders and back was announcing itself with enthusiasm this morning.

<<dialog "Mira">>
How's the leg?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Better than it should be.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Better as in 'I can hobble to the privy without screaming' or better as in 'that witch's potion is doing something unnatural?'
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The second one. The ankle is weight-bearing. The calf wound isn't hot anymore. She stitched me up twelve hours ago and it feels like it's been healing for days.
<</dialog>>

Mira sat up. Looked at me standing there   dressed, upright, weight on both feet. Her expression went through several iterations: surprise, assessment, cautious optimism, and then the particular narrowing of her eyes that meant she was filing something under "suspicious but useful."

<<dialog "Mira">>
Come back to bed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We need to figure out what we're going to do about Grent 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Aldric. Come back to bed.
<</dialog>>

That tone. The one that wasn't a request. The one that said she'd already decided what was happening and was offering me the courtesy of pretending I had a vote. I went.

She pulled me down beside her. Rolled on top of me, careful   instinctively careful, positioning her weight to avoid my injured leg, her knee sliding between mine on the good side, her hips settling against mine with the practiced precision of a woman who'd memorized my body's geography over two months of intimate navigation.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You scared me last night.
<</dialog>>

She said it quietly. The volume of a confession. The tone of someone who was only willing to admit vulnerability at a whisper, as if saying it louder would make it more real than she could tolerate.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're not fine. You have twelve stitches in your calf and an ankle the color of a plum. Your fine is a very generous interpretation of the available evidence.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm alive. I'm vertical. I'm in bed with you. All things considered, that's a good morning.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
When the floor collapsed. When I saw the blood. When you screamed.
<</dialog>>

She paused. Her amber eyes were steady on mine, but there was something behind them   a tremor, barely visible, like a hairline crack in stone. She'd been holding this since last night. Carrying it the way she'd carried me and now, in the quiet of the morning, with the danger past and the adrenaline burned off, the weight of what had almost happened was pressing on her chest the way she was pressing on mine.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I thought you were gone. For about three seconds, between the crash and when I saw you moving, I thought the fall had killed you. Three seconds. It felt like a month.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
But it didn't kill me.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
But it could have and if it had, the last thing between us would have been a blow job and some boot laces.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's not the worst epitaph I've heard.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Shut up. I'm being serious.
<</dialog>>

She kissed me. Slow this time   not the pre-job urgency of yesterday, not the focused efficiency of her usual approach to intimacy. Something softer. Grateful. The kiss of a woman who'd carried a man through the dark and was relieved beyond expression to have him here in the morning light. Her hands framed my face, thumbs on my cheekbones, fingers threading into my hair. She held me there and kissed me like she was memorizing the shape of my mouth.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I want to feel you.
<</dialog>>

She undressed us both. Carefully   mindful of the bandages, the swollen ankle, the stitches. My tunic came off with gentle hands, not the urgent pulling of their usual encounters. My trousers eased down over the injury. Her own clothes removed with the economical grace that was simply how Mira moved   no wasted motion, even in vulnerability.

She positioned herself on top. Straddling my hips, her thighs warm against mine, her weight distributed to keep pressure off the injured leg. My cock stiffened against her   the proximity, the warmth of her skin against mine, the way she was looking at me with those half-lidded amber eyes. Even through the general strangeness of the morning   the heightened sensitivity, the too-sharp textures   the familiar circuit of desire fired on schedule. She was warm and lean and naked above me and my body knew what that meant.

She reached between us. Took me in hand   the archer's grip, confident, familiar   and positioned herself. Then she sank down. Not the decisive impalement of their usual sessions. This time she went slowly. Inch by inch. Her lips parting with each increment, her breath catching in small, measured inhalations. I could feel every fraction of the descent   her body opening around me, the slick heat of her, the tight half-elf muscles gripping and yielding in alternation.

When she was fully seated, she held still. Just feeling. Her hands flat on my chest, fingers spread in the dark hair, palms against the pectorals she'd mapped a hundred times. I could feel my own heartbeat through her hands, or maybe it was hers   our pulses close enough that they blurred together.

<<dialog "Mira">>
There. That's what I needed.
<</dialog>>

She moved. Slow, deep, grinding rolls of her hips that kept me buried inside her and moved me within her rather than in and out. Less fucking and more communion. Connection. The physical language of you're alive and whole and here and I can feel you. Her clit pressed against my pelvis with each grind, and she bit her lower lip   the expression of focused sensation, chasing the feeling without rushing it.

I held her hips and let her lead. My thumbs traced circles on her hipbones   the sharp architecture of her, lean muscle over prominent bone, the body of a weapon I'd learned to hold. She was built for speed and distance. I was built for weight and endurance. Together we made something that shouldn't have worked but did   angles against curves, rough against smooth, two broken pieces from different sets that somehow fit.

She came quietly. A shudder, a long exhale through her nose, her cunt tightening around me in slow rolling contractions. She dropped forward, her forehead against my chest, breathing hard, her lips against my skin.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your turn.
<</dialog>>

I thrust up   gently, slowly, controlled by the need to keep weight off the ankle. She clenched around me deliberately   that archer's muscle control, the precision squeeze and release she knew drove me over. It didn't take long. I came inside her with a low groan, hands tightening on her hips, pulling her flush, emptying into the clenching warmth while she held perfectly still and let me finish.

Five minutes of silence. Breathing. Her head on my chest. My arm around her back. The morning light painting stripes across the wall.

Then Mira sat up, pulled the sheet around her shoulders, and became a different person. The transition from intimate to operational happened in the space between one blink and the next. 

<<dialog "Mira">>
Right. We need a plan.
<</dialog>>
<<addobjective "day_3" "Deal with Grent and the Consortium">>
[[Make a plan|day_3_voss]]

:: day_3_2_a
<<changestat "mira"  -2>>
I dressed and went downstairs alone. Let Mira sleep   she'd earned it, and one of us should be functional today.

The common room was quiet. Early morning hush   just the innkeeper behind the bar, his face buried in the perpetual ledger, and a stable hand eating porridge at a corner table with the focused determination of a man fueling up for work. The orange cat was on the bar, cleaning its paw. The old man in the far corner was, as always, asleep.

I ordered breakfast. Two coppers. Bread, hard cheese, weak ale. The bread was yesterday's bread, which meant it was actually the day before yesterday's bread with a fresh opinion of itself. The cheese had the personality of a brick. The ale tasted like someone had described the concept of ale to a glass of water and hoped for the best.

I ate in the corner booth. Our booth. The one with the high backs and the sightlines and the comfortable familiarity of a place I'd sat in every day for a week.

My hands on the table looked the same. Big, scarred across the knuckles, calluses thick on the palms from a decade of swordwork. But when I pressed my fingertips against the rough wood of the table, the texture was louder than it should have been. More detailed. Each grain of the wood, each splinter, each groove   vivid, specific, like my fingertips had been recalibrated to a higher sensitivity.

I flexed my hands. Made fists. Released. The calluses were there. The scars were there. Everything the same.

I ate my breakfast and waited for Mira.

[[Make a plan|day_3_voss]]


:: day_3_voss [main_story]

We were in the corner booth   our booth, the one with the high backs and the sightlines   finishing breakfast that didn't deserve the name. The bread had opinions about its own freshness that the bread had not earned. The cheese was older than some of my scars. The ale was a theological argument against the existence of a benevolent god.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need to deal with Grent.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Today. Before he finds us. Because if he finds us first, the conversation starts with him angry and ends with us negotiating from the floor. If we find him first   or better, if we find his people first   we control the terms.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
His people.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Voss. The accounts woman at the Consortium warehouse. Grent's a field operator   he recruits, he contracts, he collects. But the books are Voss's. If we can get Voss to restructure the debt before Grent comes knocking, we walk into that conversation with an arrangement already in place. He can't overrule his own accountant. Nobody overrules the person who knows where the money is.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You want to go to the Consortium warehouse. Right now. With me limping like a three-legged horse and our total assets amounting to 
<</dialog>>

I counted the coins in my belt pouch by feel. The small, dispiriting math of poverty conducted through fingertips.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
 less than what that breakfast cost.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We're not going to pay. We're going to negotiate. There's a difference.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The difference is vocabulary.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The difference is leverage and I have some.
<</dialog>>

She said it with the particular confidence of a woman who'd been thinking about this since before dawn   possibly since before sleep, possibly since the moment we'd crawled out of the Greymire mine empty-handed and she'd started running the numbers on how badly we were about to be screwed. Mira's mind worked like her bow   the arrow was already in flight before anyone else heard the string release.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What leverage?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The mine. We didn't bring back cargo, but we brought back intelligence. Layout, warband size, tunnel conditions, storage locations. The Consortium wants those crates recovered   they'll send another team. A better-equipped one and that team will need exactly the kind of information we have. Tunnel maps. Entry and exit points. Guard rotation patterns. Goblin numbers and positioning.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We were fighting for our lives in the dark. I didn't exactly stop to take notes.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I did.
<</dialog>>

She reached into her vest and produced a folded piece of parchment   Grent's napkin map from the briefing, annotated in Mira's small, precise handwriting. She'd added details. Tunnel widths. Junction angles. The location of the collapse. The storage shaft where the crates were stacked. Goblin camp location, estimated warband size (eighteen to twenty-two, she'd written, with the confident specificity of someone who'd counted while killing them). Even the sentry position outside the entrance.

She'd done this in the dark. While carrying me through a forest. Between stitching my leg and falling asleep in a chair with her bow on her knees.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
When did you 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
While you were unconscious. Your snoring is very productive   it gives me time to think.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I don't snore.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You snore like a bear with a sinus infection. It's one of your less charming qualities and it gave me two uninterrupted hours to reconstruct the mine layout from memory and calculate what that information is worth to the Consortium. The answer is: more than twenty-two silver.
<</dialog>>

She folded the map. Tucked it back into her vest.

<<dialog "Mira">>
That's the opener. The intelligence pays down the debt. But we need something else   active work. A contract that generates revenue while clearing the books. If Voss has an escort job or a survey contract that needs bodies, we offer ourselves at a discounted rate against the outstanding balance. The Consortium gets cheap labor. We get clear of the debt. Everyone's ledger balances.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
If Voss doesn't have a contract?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She will. The Consortium always has contracts. That's what the Consortium does   it moves things, and moving things requires people to protect the things being moved. Somewhere in that warehouse is a job that needs two sellswords who'll work for debt relief instead of silver. We just have to find it before Grent finds us.
<</dialog>>

She stood. Pulled on her vest. Slung her bow. The transformation from bedmate to business partner was complete   the woman who'd whispered confessions into my chest was gone, replaced by the half-elf operative who'd mapped a goblin mine from memory while I snored.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Dockside. Now. Can you walk?
<</dialog>>

I tested the ankle. It complained   the stiff, grinding protest of a joint that was healing but hadn't forgiven me yet. The calf wound throbbed beneath its bandages. But both held weight. Both functioned. Whatever Ysolde's green vial was doing to me, it was doing it fast.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I can walk.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then walk. We've got an accountant to charm.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You don't charm people, Mira. You present them with a cost-benefit analysis and let their survival instinct do the rest.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That is charm. It's just the honest version.
<</dialog>>
<<set $day_3_voss_warehouse = false>>
<<link "Go to the Dockside warehouse">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_3_voss_warehouse [main_story]
<<set $day_3_voss_warehouse = true>>
The Consortium warehouse on Dockside Row was massive   old stone and iron-reinforced doors, built to survive floods, fires, and the general indignity of housing other people's valuables in a city that considered theft a competitive sport. The scales-and-wheel emblem was painted across the front wall in blue and gold that had faded to something closer to gray and mustard, the paint peeling in strips that the sea air had been working on for decades.

Two guards flanked the entrance. Armed. Bored. The particular boredom of men whose job was to look threatening enough to prevent situations that would require them to actually be threatening. They glanced at my sword, at Mira's bow, and decided we weren't worth the paperwork.

Inside, the warehouse was organized chaos   a phrase I'd learned in Thornwall and which applied to every Consortium facility I'd ever entered. Crates stacked in labeled rows, manifests pinned to every vertical surface, dock workers hauling cargo along chalk-marked lanes like ants in a colony whose queen was obsessed with inventory management. The air smelled of salt, rope fiber, lamp oil, and the particular dusty sweetness of sealed wooden crates stacked too close together for too long.

A clerk at a desk near the entrance looked up with the practiced expression of a man who sorted visitors into categories: important, unimportant, and actively annoying. We apparently fell into the third category.

<<dialog "Clerk">>
Business?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need to see Voss. Accounts office.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Clerk">>
Regarding?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The Greymire mine contract. Disbursement reference: Grent, field operations. Outstanding balance on the advance.
<</dialog>>

She said it with the practiced fluency of someone who'd learned Consortium vocabulary the way she'd learned goblin tracking   by necessity, through exposure, with the particular efficiency of a survivor who treated every institution's language as a dialect to be mastered and deployed.

The clerk's expression shifted. Not warmer. The particular careful blankness of a man who recognized the name attached to a problem and wanted the problem to ascend to someone else's office as quickly as possible.

<<dialog "Clerk">>
Upstairs. Second door. She's expecting you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
She's expecting us?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Clerk">>
She's always expecting people. It's what makes her terrifying.
<</dialog>>

We went upstairs. The staircase was narrow, stone, designed for function rather than comfort   the architectural philosophy of a building that considered its occupants an inconvenience to be tolerated rather than a population to be served. My ankle negotiated each step with the grim determination of a joint that had been asked to do something unreasonable and was complying under protest.

The accounts office was at the top. A cramped room overlooking the warehouse floor through a single window that was more ledge than aperture, lit by an oil lamp that burned with the steady, joyless flame of a light source that had never been asked to be atmospheric. The room was dominated by a desk buried under ledgers   not scattered, not messy, but layered. Geological strata of paperwork, each layer representing a different era of Consortium financial history, compressed by the weight of subsequent transactions into something approaching sedimentary rock.

Behind the desk sat Voss.

Mid-thirties. Sharp-featured   the word "sharp" doing a lot of work here, applying equally to her cheekbones, her nose, her jaw, and the expression she wore, which could have been used to cut glass. Spectacles perched on a narrow nose, the lenses slightly smudged with ink. Her fingers were stained   blue-black at the tips, the permanent badge of a woman who lived in ink the way soldiers lived in leather. Her hair was dark, pulled back in a knot so tight it seemed to be stretching her face into its current expression of institutional disappointment.

She didn't look up when we entered. Her quill was moving   a steady, metronomic scratch across the page of the open ledger, each stroke precise, each number formed with the care of someone who understood that a misplaced digit could destabilize an empire.

We stood. Waiting. The silence was strategic   Voss's silence, deployed the way Mira deployed arrows. With patience, precision, and the absolute confidence that the target would present itself if she waited long enough.

She finished the column. Set down the quill. Opened a specific ledger to a specific page without searching   the page was marked with a red ribbon, which was either a filing system or a warning.

<<dialog "Voss">>
Aldric Vane and companion. Greymire mine recovery contract. Twenty silver advance disbursed. Zero cargo recovered. Zero crates delivered. Outstanding balance: twenty silver, plus two silver administrative fee for contract non-completion. Twenty-two silver total.
<</dialog>>

She read it like weather   impersonal, factual, the data existing independent of any opinion about the data.

<<dialog "Voss">>
Interest accrues at one silver per week beginning five days from initial disbursement. Which means 
<</dialog>>

She glanced at a calendar pinned to the wall. Counted.

<<dialog "Voss">>
 you have two days before the first interest charge applies. After which your twenty-two silver becomes twenty-three and then twenty-four and so on, until the Consortium's collections department becomes involved, at which point the arithmetic becomes significantly less pleasant.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Collections department.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
The Consortium has a long institutional memory and a very efficient collections infrastructure. I trust we won't need to involve them. But I don't control their deployment   I only control the numbers that trigger it and your numbers, Mr. Vane, are trending in the wrong direction.
<</dialog>>

She folded her hands on the ledger. Looked at us   at me, specifically   with the evaluating gaze of a woman who'd assessed ten thousand debtors and found nine thousand of them wanting. Her eyes traveled from my face to my bandaged leg to my sword to my scarred hands and back to my face, and the entire assessment took about three seconds.

<<dialog "Voss">>
You don't look like a man who has twenty-two silver.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Then why are you in my office? Debtors who can't pay usually avoid the accounts department, not visit it voluntarily. Which means you want something. People who want something from the Consortium always want the same thing   time, terms, or forgiveness. I don't offer forgiveness. The Consortium doesn't believe in it. So: time or terms?
<</dialog>>

Mira stepped forward. The napkin map appeared in her hand   the annotated mine layout, dense with her precise notations, the product of two hours of reconstruction conducted while I snored and bled.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Terms. We're offering a two-part arrangement. First: intelligence on the Greymire mine. Layout, warband size, tunnel conditions, storage locations, entry points, guard positions. Everything you need to send a recovery team that actually comes back with your cargo.
<</dialog>>

She unfolded the map on Voss's desk. The annotated details were visible   junction angles, tunnel widths, the storage shaft, the collapse point, goblin numbers. Voss's eyes dropped to it. Her pupils didn't dilate   Voss's pupils probably didn't dilate for anything short of a mathematical proof   but her attention sharpened. The shift from tolerating our presence to evaluating our product.

<<dialog "Voss">>
You mapped the mine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
From memory. In the field. Under combat conditions. The warband was eighteen to twenty-two strong   I counted kills and remaining hostiles during extraction. The storage shaft is forty paces from the main junction, angled down, accessible through a secondary tunnel that branches left at a pictograph marker. Four crates were sealed when we left. Two more had intact contents. The rest were compromised.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
You're thorough.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm a professional. The intelligence is worth more than your administrative fee. Apply it against the balance.
<</dialog>>

Voss looked at the map for a long moment. Her fingers   the ink-stained ones, the fingers that lived in ledgers   traced the tunnel layout, the notations, the goblin positions. She was reading Mira's handwriting the way she read financial columns   for accuracy, for consistency, for the internal logic that distinguished real intelligence from fabrication.

Whatever she found passed her test.

<<dialog "Voss">>
Five silver credit against the outstanding balance. The intelligence is useful but unverified. If the recovery team confirms your data, I'll apply an additional three. That brings you to fourteen remaining   or eleven if the data checks out.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The second part. Active work. The Consortium has contracts that need bodies. Escort jobs, surveys, security   the kind of work that requires armed professionals and pays in silver. We're offering to take a contract at discounted rate, payment applied directly against the balance. The Consortium gets cheap labor. We get clear of the debt. The ledger balances.
<</dialog>>

Voss removed her spectacles. Cleaned them on a cloth she kept in a desk drawer   a deliberate, measured gesture that bought her thinking time while appearing merely fastidious. She held the spectacles up to the lamp light. Examined them for smudges. Found one. Cleaned it.

<<dialog "Voss">>
The Consortium has an escort contract pending. Surveyor to a ruins site east of the city. Three-day round trip through territory that has been... unpredictable lately. The original escort withdrew due to   let's call it a change in risk assessment.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What kind of change?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
The kind that involves goblins. The same warband you encountered at the mine may have secondary camps along the eastern road. Our surveyor is a Consortium assessor evaluating a potential acquisition site. He requires armed escort. The contract pays fifteen silver each on completion.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fifteen each plus the intelligence credit. That's thirty plus five   thirty-five silver against a twenty-two silver debt. We clear the books and walk away with thirteen silver profit.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
If you complete the contract. If the surveyor returns unharmed. If the assessment data is delivered intact.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If, if, if. The Consortium loves conditionals. They let you promise everything and deliver nothing.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
The Consortium loves certainty. Conditionals are what we use when certainty isn't available. Which, in your case 
<</dialog>>

Her eyes went to my bandaged leg again. The professional assessment of a woman who managed risk for a living and was looking at a risk factor wrapped in linen and favoring its right side.

<<dialog "Voss">>
 it isn't.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'll be ready.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
You'll need to be. The surveyor departs on the fourth morning from today. I'll send the route details and assessment parameters to your lodging. The Broken Antler, I assume   it's where all the sellswords who can't afford better end up.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You know where we're staying.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
I know where everyone who owes the Consortium money is staying. It's part of the service.
<</dialog>>

She replaced her spectacles. Opened the ledger. Made a notation   precise, small, the quill scratching against the paper with the sound of a future being written. She didn't show us what she'd written. The number was between her and the book.

<<dialog "Voss">>
The advance repayment is suspended pending contract completion. The intelligence credit of five silver is applied immediately. If Mr. Grent contacts you before the escort departs   which he will, because Mr. Grent has the patience of a wasp in a bottle   you may inform him that the arrangement has been approved at the accounts level. He won't like it. But he'll accept it. Mr. Grent always accepts what the accounts department decides. That's why the accounts department exists.
<</dialog>>

She picked up her quill. The dismissal was absolute   not a gesture, not a word, just the resumption of work, which communicated more clearly than any spoken farewell that we were no longer the most interesting thing in her immediate environment. We had been processed. Filed. Categorized. The debt had a plan attached to it now, and Voss had moved on to the next column of figures the way a river moves past a stone it's already worn smooth.

We left.

On the stairs, Mira exhaled   a long, controlled breath, the release of tension she'd been holding since we walked through the warehouse door. She leaned against the stairwell wall, closed her eyes, and tipped her head back.

<<dialog "Mira">>
That could have gone worse.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You had the map. You had the pitch. You had the whole thing planned before breakfast.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I had it planned before dinner last night. While you were bleeding on a table in the Cistern District, I was reconstructing tunnel layouts in my head and calculating the intelligence value per data point. It's how I cope. Some people drink. Some people pray. I do math.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's the most Mira thing you've ever said.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know. I'm a brand.
<</dialog>>

She opened her eyes. Looked at me. The amber gaze   warm in the stairwell's lamplight, gold-edged, the eyes that had been watching me sleep and carrying me through the dark and mapping goblin mines from memory while I bled.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Three days. We have three days to get your leg working, prepare for the escort, and avoid Grent until the arrangement is official.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
About Grent 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He'll find us. He always does. But now when he does, we have Voss's arrangement as a shield. He can't override the accounts department. Nobody overrides the person who knows where the money is.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You said that before.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Because it's the truest thing I know about how power works. Swords are impressive. Muscles are useful. But the person with the ledger runs the world, and everyone else is just providing content for the entries.
<</dialog>>

We emerged from the warehouse into the Dockside air   salt, tar, fish guts, the perpetual olfactory punishment of a district that existed to move cargo and didn't care about the comfort of the people doing the moving. The morning was gray and damp. The dock workers were already at it, hauling and shouting and living their lives in the spaces between the crates.

My leg hurt. The walk to Dockside had cost me   the ankle was swelling again inside the boot, the calf wound sending sharp reminders with every stride that healing wasn't the same as healed. But the debt had a structure now. A plan. A woman with ink-stained fingers and a ledger had written something down, and whatever she'd written had the weight of institutional authority behind it.

We walked back to the Broken Antler. Slowly. My pace dictating, Mira matching it without complaint, without comment, without the passive-aggressive velocity she deployed when she thought I was being slow for emotional rather than medical reasons. This time the slowness was legitimate and she respected it the way she respected all legitimate limitations   with patience and the quiet understanding that the body would deliver what the body could deliver, and pushing harder would cost more than waiting.

We were halfway through the Market District when Mira said it.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Voss is interesting.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Interesting how?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Interesting in the way that people who control information are always interesting. She knew where we were staying. She knew Grent would find us. She had the escort contract ready   not because we asked for it, but because she'd already identified us as potential assets and was waiting for us to walk in so she could deploy us. We didn't negotiate. We were handled.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You sound impressed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm impressed by anyone who's better at strategy than I am. It happens rarely, so I notice when it does. Voss is playing a game that's three moves ahead of where we're standing. I want to know what game.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The Consortium's game.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Maybe. Or maybe her own. People like Voss   people who sit in rooms full of numbers and see the patterns underneath   they always have their own game. The institution is just the board they play it on.
<</dialog>>

She let the thought settle. We kept walking. The Broken Antler appeared at the end of the street   squat, familiar, smelling of pipe smoke and the accumulated misery of a building that had been hosting other people's bad days since before either of us was born.
<<set $day_3_3 = false>>
<<link "Go to the Broken Antler">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_3_3
<<set $day_3_3 = true>>
We went inside. Ordered ale. Sat in our booth and waited for Grent. Finally he came. He filled the doorframe the way a cork fills a bottle. Oilskin cloak dripping, scar-tissue face arranged in the expression of a man who'd come to collect and wasn't in the mood for conversation. His small eyes found us in the corner booth with the targeting speed of someone who'd been looking for exactly two broke mercenaries and had found them precisely where broke mercenaries tended to sit.

He crossed the common room in four strides that shook the floorboards and sat down across from us without invitation. Again. The same deliberate placement of his bulk. The same territorial claiming of space.

<<dialog "Grent">>
You hit the mine.
<</dialog>>

Not a question. Grent's information network was apparently as efficient as the rest of him.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We hit the mine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
You came out empty-handed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We came out alive. Which, given that the warband was closer to twenty than fifteen, I consider a professional achievement.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
The Consortium considers recovered cargo a professional achievement. Breathing is a baseline expectation, not a bonus. I want my advance back. Twenty silver.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We don't have twenty silver.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Then we have a problem.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We have a solution. We spoke with Voss at the Dockside warehouse today morning. 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
You spoke with Voss.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I spoke with Voss. She's agreed to waive the advance repayment contingent on our completion of a secondary contract. Escort job   surveyor to a ruins site east of here. Three days, round trip. Fifteen silver each on completion, plus the debt cleared. The Consortium gets its money's worth. You get your contract fulfilled. We get to eat next week. Everyone wins.
<</dialog>>

Grent looked at her. Then at me. His eyes traveled down to my leg   the bandaged calf, the swollen ankle visible above my boot line, the general state of someone who'd been through a meat grinder and come out the cheap end.

<<dialog "Grent">>
You look like hell.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You should see the goblins.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
I'm not interested in the goblins. I'm interested in whether a sellsword who can barely stand is going to be able to escort a Consortium surveyor through bandit territory without getting himself or the surveyor killed. Because if the answer is no, then this arrangement wastes everyone's time and I still want my twenty silver.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He'll be ready. The leg is healing fast. Give us three days and he'll be mobile.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Three days.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Three days. We depart on the fourth morning with the surveyor, complete the escort, deliver Voss's cargo assessment, and square the books. You get your money's worth of mercenary labor. We get clear of the debt. Clean and simple.
<</dialog>>

Grent's jaw moved   the grinding motion of a man chewing on a decision. He looked at my leg one more time. Looked at my face. Something shifted behind those small, calculating eyes   the mental arithmetic of a man who dealt in risk and reward, balancing the cost of chasing twenty silver from two broke sellswords against the potential return of getting free labor.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Voss will be in touch with the escort details. Don't make me come back here.
<</dialog>>

He stood. The chair scraped against the floor. He pulled his oilskin hood up and left without another word, the door banging shut behind him with a finality that rattled the windows.

The orange cat, disturbed by the bang, leaped off the bar and stalked away with the offended dignity of a creature that had been deeply inconvenienced.

<<dialog "Mira">>
That went well.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
He looked like he wanted to break my other leg.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He looked like he wanted to break both your legs AND your arms. But he didn't, which means Voss's arrangement holds. Crisis managed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
For now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
For now is all we've ever had. I'm going to the Consortium warehouse to confirm the escort details. You   stay here. Rest the leg. Every hour of healing matters.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What am I supposed to do sitting in a room all day?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Not die. Not reopen your stitches. Not make any financial commitments. The bar is low. I believe in you.
<</dialog>>

She squeezed my shoulder   the same brief, compressed gesture of affection that was her substitute for everything a normal person might express with words   and left. The tavern door closed behind her with significantly less violence than Grent's departure, which was Mira's version of being gentle.
<<addobjective "day_3" "Rest and recover">>
[[Alone in the room...|day_3_4]]

:: day_3_4

The room was quiet in a way it hadn't been since we arrived in Valdmere. Mira was gone   out negotiating our survival at the Consortium warehouse, doing the work she was better at than me, which was most work that didn't involve swinging a sword. I was alone with the lumpy bed, the horse-shaped water stain, and the growing suspicion that something was happening to my body that I couldn't explain.

I sat on the edge of the bed. Unwrapped the calf bandage. The wound was   I stared at it. Blinked. Stared again.

The twelve sutures Ysolde had placed with such meticulous care were sitting in skin that looked like it had been healing for a week. The angry redness was gone. The swelling had reduced by half. The edges of the wound were closed, the skin around them pink and healthy, the kind of healthy that suggested new growth rather than inflammation. The stitches themselves were almost ready to come out   the tissue had knit together underneath them with a speed that made no medical sense.

This was three or four days of healing compressed into less than twenty-four hours.

I touched the area around the wound. Gentle pressure. No heat. No tenderness. No sign of the infection that had been setting in when Ysolde extracted those eight splinters from my muscle last night. Whatever her compound was doing, it was doing it with the focused efficiency of an entire temple of healers working in shifts.

I rewrapped the bandage and sat by the window. The market district was busy below   a normal day in Valdmere. Carts rattling over cobblestones. Merchants adjusting their stall displays. A group of children chasing a spotted dog through the crowd, their laughter high and sharp and uncomplicated by the kind of problems that adults accumulated like barnacles on a hull.

Normal life. The kind of life that cost money and required stability and assumed a body that stayed the same from one day to the next.

My hands were resting on the windowsill. I looked at them.

Big hands. A swordsman's hands. Wide palms, thick fingers, calluses layered over calluses   the hard ridges of keratin that ten years of gripping steel had built up like geological strata. Scars across the knuckles from fights I'd won and fights I'd lost and a few that had ended in draws where both participants walked away bloody and claimed victory to different audiences. The calluses were thinner.

I pressed my thumb against the rough wood of the windowsill and the texture hit me like a shout. Every grain, every splinter, every imperfection in the wood   vivid and specific, as if someone had polished the nerve endings in my fingertips to a higher resolution. I could feel details I'd never noticed before. The subtle ridges of the wood grain. The difference in temperature between the sunlit spots and the shaded ones. The faint vibration of a cart rolling past on the street below, transmitted through the building's structure and into the windowsill and into my skin.

I flexed my hands. Opened. Closed. They looked the same. Almost. The calluses were there but their edges were softer, as if they'd been sanded down overnight. The rough skin of my palms was smoother to the touch   still a working man's hands, still scarred and thick-fingered, but with a new layer of sensitivity underneath the toughness, like wearing thin gloves you could feel through. I dropped my hands. Shook them out.

After-effects. The compound was a powerful healing agent and it was working on everything   not just the wound, but the surrounding tissue, the skin, the nerves. Heightened sensitivity was a side effect. A temporary one. It would fade as the compound worked its way through my system.

[[Time passes...|day_3_5]]

:: day_3_5

I spent an hour sitting by that window, flexing my hands and trying not to think. I failed at the not-thinking part. The brain is a poor servant when you tell it not to examine something   the instruction itself becomes an obsession, the forbidden thought growing larger and more detailed the harder you push it away. Don't think about your skin. Now all I could feel was my skin. Don't think about your calluses. Now my palms were the center of my universe.

I stood up. Walked across the room. Walked back. The ankle protested   a dull ache, manageable, improving by the hour. The calf wound was a distant memory of pain, the stitches pulling slightly with each step but nothing more.

My belt felt different. I stopped. Looked down. Tugged at the leather. It was at the same notch   the one I'd been using since Thornwall, the comfortable middle position that accommodated a full stomach and an empty one. But it was marginally looser. Not enough to slip. Not enough that I'd notice in normal circumstances. But I was not in normal circumstances and I was noticing everything, and the belt was sitting a fraction lower on my hips than it had yesterday.

Weight loss. I'd bled. I'd barely eaten. Blood loss and reduced appetite equaled weight fluctuation. Normal. Explainable.

I rubbed my jaw. The stubble was there   rough under my fingers, the scratch of three days' growth. But one spot along my upper jaw, just below the sideburn, was smooth. Not smooth like a missed spot shaving. Smooth like nothing was growing there. I pressed, feeling for the follicles, the tiny bumps that indicated hair would return. Nothing. Just skin, soft and even.

I pulled up my tunic and looked at my chest. The pectorals were the same   thick, hard, the flat planes of muscle that came from years of sword work. The dark hair was there, concentrated in the center with a trail running down my midline toward my navel. But was it thinner? I tried to remember what my chest hair looked like yesterday, the day before, last week. You don't catalog your own chest hair. You don't pay attention to the specific density of growth on your pectorals unless someone gives you a reason to.

No one had given me a reason. Yet. I dropped the tunic. Turned to the mirror. Leaned close.

My face. The same face. Square jaw, green eyes, scar through the eyebrow. The same face I'd worn for twenty-six years, the face that looked back at me from every polished shield and every still pond and every lover's eyes.

Was it softer?

I pressed my fingers against my cheekbone. Against my jaw. Against the ridge of my brow. The bones were the same. The structure was identical. But the skin over them   smoother. The weathered, sun-roughened texture that had been my face's defining characteristic for years was... diminished. Not gone. Just less. Like the difference between old parchment and new parchment   same material, different miles.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's the healing compound. It's a side effect. It'll pass.
<</dialog>>

I said it to the mirror. The mirror didn't argue, which I took as agreement even though mirrors aren't qualified to practice medicine.
<<set $day_3_6_a = false>>
[[Go downstairs...|broken_antler]]
[[Stay inside room|day_3_4_b]]

:: day_3_6_a
<<set $day_3_6_a = true>>
Boredom drove me downstairs. Boredom and a thirst that bad ale could technically satisfy, and the need to be somewhere other than a small room with a small mirror and a growing collection of questions I didn't want to ask.

The common room was drowsy. Afternoon light slanting through the grimy windows, dust motes drifting in the beams like tiny lost travelers. A few day-drinkers at the bar. The innkeeper sweeping in his usual perfunctory manner   the broom moving, technically, but the dirt's strategic position remaining unchanged. Lenna was at the bar.

Not working   afternoon off, casual clothes. The linen blouse buttoned to the collar, hair pulled back in the practical knot. No bodice, no presentation. The daylight version of her, which was simpler and younger and somehow more herself than the candlelit version had been.

She was eating bread with honey and drinking tea, and she spotted my limp before she spotted my face.

<<dialog "Lenna">>
Gods, Aldric. What happened to your leg?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Goblin mine. Floor collapsed. I went through it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
The job from the other night? The one you wouldn't tell me about?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's the one. It went about as well as you'd expect a job I wouldn't talk about to go.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
You should be resting.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm bad at resting. Resting requires sitting still and thinking, and right now my thoughts are not company I'd choose voluntarily.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
Then sit with me. I'm better company than your thoughts, and the tea is free if you flirt with the innkeeper's wife.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The innkeeper has a wife?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
The innkeeper has a wife, two children, and a collection of ceramic birds that he keeps in the back room. He's a deeply complicated man under all that indifference. Sit down.
<</dialog>>

I sat. She flagged the barmaid and ordered me an ale   her money, not mine. A reversal of their previous transaction that made something warm shift in my chest.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You don't have to 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
You bought an hour. I can buy an ale. Fair trade. Drink it.
<</dialog>>

The ale was the same terrible swill the Antler always served, but it tasted better coming from someone else's coin. We sat together at the bar, her tea steaming, my ale flat, the afternoon light making the common room almost pleasant if you squinted past the grime.

She told me about herself. Not the professional version   the real one. Father was a fisherman who worked the Valdmere harbor until his back gave out. Mother died when Lenna was twelve   the coughing sickness, the one that took the poor and the crowded first. She'd started at the Antler at seventeen because the innkeeper's wife took pity on her and the work was steady and the customers were rarely worse than rude.

<<dialog "Lenna">>
I'm saving. Every extra copper goes into a box under my bed. When I have enough, I'm opening a seamstress shop. I'm good with a needle   my mother taught me before she got sick. I can do repairs, alterations, basic tailoring. Nothing fancy but solid work. The kind of work that feeds you without requiring you to take your clothes off.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How close are you?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
Another year. Maybe two. The rent on a shop in the lower market is seven silver a month, and I need a three-month deposit plus materials. It adds up.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You'll get there.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
I will. Stubbornness runs in my family. My father fished the harbor for thirty years even after his back went bad. He'd go out in the morning hunched over like a question mark and come back at night with enough fish to feed us. Stubbornness is the poor person's talent. It doesn't cost anything and it works when everything else fails.
<</dialog>>

I told her about Dunmore. The farm. The lord who took the land   not through battle or conquest, but through paperwork and taxes and the slow, grinding bureaucracy of power applied to people too small to fight it. My father drinking himself into a grave after the fields that had been in his family for three generations were signed away by a clerk who'd never set foot on them. My mother moving in with her sister. My brother apprenticing to a blacksmith. Me, taking up the sword because it was the one trade that didn't require land or a guild membership or a willingness to pretend the world was fair.

<<dialog "Lenna">>
We're the same, you and me. Different trade, same story. People who lost the ground under their feet and had to build something new from nothing.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're building something. I'm just swinging a sword and hoping the money lasts until the next job.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Lenna">>
That's building too. Different materials, same project. Survival is construction, Aldric. Every day you're alive, you've built another floor on the building.
<</dialog>>

She squeezed my hand when she left. Her fingers were warm, the callus on her thumb pressing against my knuckle   and the sensation was sharper than it should have been, the skin-to-skin contact registering with a vividness that made me blink.

<<dialog "Lenna">>
Rest, Aldric. Whatever that woman gave you for the leg, let it work.
<</dialog>>

She walked away. I watched her go. The freckles on the back of her neck, visible where the hair was pulled up. Copper constellations on warm skin. I wanted to count them, which was a stupid thing to want, and yet.

I finished my ale. Went back upstairs. Sat by the window. Flexed my hands on the windowsill and felt every grain of the wood with a clarity that was becoming harder to explain.

[[Mira returns...|day_3_7]]

:: day_3_4_b

Mira was gone. The room was mine, and the silence was the kind that invites investigation. I sat on the bed. Looked at my hands again. The calluses were definitely thinner   I wasn't imagining it. The hard ridges I'd built over a decade of swordwork were softening at the edges, the skin underneath smoother and more tender than I'd ever felt it. When I gripped the edge of the bed frame, the wood felt different in my palm   more detailed, more present, as if my hand was learning a new language of texture.

I stood and walked to the mirror. Checked my jaw again. The smooth spot below the sideburn was still there   a patch of bare skin where stubble should have been. I searched for others. Found one on my chin, slightly to the left of center. The hair around it was thinner than the rest, the three-day growth barely a shadow.

I pulled my tunic up. Looked at my chest. The pectorals were unchanged   thick, hard, the flat slabs of muscle that I'd been carrying since my teens. But the hair over them was thinner. I could see more skin through it than yesterday. Individual hairs seemed finer, lighter, as if they were in retreat   not gone, but diminishing, like a forest thinning at the edges.

I touched my right nipple. Not deliberately   my hand grazed it while pulling the tunic down, an accidental contact that shouldn't have registered. It registered.

A jolt of sensation shot from the nipple straight through my chest and into my stomach and down into my groin, a hot wire of feeling that made me inhale sharply through my teeth. Not pain. Not pleasure, exactly. Something in between   a sensitivity so intense that the lightest touch was overwhelming, the nerve endings screaming at a volume they'd never used before.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What the fuck.
<</dialog>>

I said it to the empty room. The room didn't answer. I touched the other nipple. Deliberately this time. Just a fingertip, the lightest possible pressure.

The same jolt. Electric. Immediate. A sensation that traveled from the nipple through internal pathways I didn't know I had, connecting to places in my body that had never been connected to my chest before. My stomach clenched. My groin tightened. My breathing went shallow.

That had never happened. Not from a casual touch. Not from pressure that light. My nipples had always been just... there. Functional parts of anatomy that served no particular purpose and generated no particular sensation. Now they were live wires, hypersensitive to the point of being distracting.

I dropped my tunic. Pressed my palms flat against my thighs and breathed slowly until the sensation faded. It took longer than it should have. The echoes of it lingered in my chest like the ring of a struck bell, slowly diminishing but refusing to go entirely silent.

The compound. Ysolde's vial. Whatever was healing my leg at superhuman speed was also doing something to my nervous system   rewiring connections, amplifying responses, changing the way my body processed sensation. It was healing me and altering me at the same time, and I was sitting in a rented room in a bad tavern watching it happen in real time and unable to do anything about it except catalog the changes and tell myself they were temporary.

They had to be temporary. I sat by the window. Watched the street. Waited for Mira. Tried very hard not to touch my chest.
<<addobjective "day_3" "Wait for Mira to return">>
[[Mira returns...|day_3_7]]

:: day_3_7

The afternoon shadows were lengthening across the market district when I heard her boots on the stairs. The particular rhythm of Mira's walk   quick, precise, two steps at a time   was as familiar as my own heartbeat. I knew before the door opened whether she was carrying good news or bad by the speed of her approach. Tonight she was moving at "mixed news" pace   neither the brisk efficiency of a problem solved nor the measured deliberation of a disaster in progress.

The door opened. She came in carrying a canvas bag of supplies and the expression of a woman who had negotiated hard, won most of what she wanted, and was tired enough to acknowledge it.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good news and complicated news. Which do you want first?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Good news. I've had enough complicated for one day.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The escort job is confirmed. Voss signed the contract   fifteen silver each on completion, advance debt wiped clean. We depart in three days with the surveyor. His name is Hadden Marsh. He's a fussy little man with spectacles and ink-stained fingers who looks like he's never been outdoors voluntarily.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The complicated news?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The route.
<</dialog>>

She set the supply bag on the bed and pulled out a map   a real one, purchased from a cartographer's stall, marked with roads and settlements and the kind of topographical detail that turned "somewhere east" into a landscape of specific choices and specific dangers.

She unfolded it on the bed. Her finger traced a line from Valdmere eastward.

<<dialog "Mira">>
The surveyor's ruins site is two days east on the Ashenmoor Road. We escort him there, wait while he does his catalogs, escort him back. Three days total. Straightforward.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That doesn't sound complicated.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The Ashenmoor Road. Aldric. The same road we took to the Greymire mine. The same road where the Consortium's caravan was hit. The same road that a goblin warband of twenty-odd has been raiding for weeks.
<</dialog>>

She tapped the map. The escarpment where the mine sat was marked   a small notation in the cartographer's precise hand, labeled "Greymire (abandoned)." It was four leagues east of Valdmere. The surveyor's ruins site was marked further along the same road   another six leagues beyond the mine. The route passed directly through the warband's known operating range.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We killed maybe eight in the mine. Maybe ten. That leaves a dozen, give or take, who are angry and territorial and who just watched two sellswords carve through their home and walk out alive. Goblins remember faces, Aldric and they remember the ones attached to bodies that killed their kin.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We'll be on the road, not in the mine. They hit caravans   ambush and retreat. Two armed escorts with a single light-travel surveyor aren't the same target as a loaded cargo wagon.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Maybe. Or maybe a warband that just lost a third of its fighters is desperate enough to hit anything that moves through their territory, regardless of how armed it looks. Desperation makes goblins creative and creative goblins are the dangerous kind.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're saying it's too risky.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm saying it's the only job we have, the route goes through hostile territory, and you'll be walking it on a leg that was impaled two days ago. I'm not saying it's too risky. I'm saying the risk needs to be managed, and managing it starts with knowing exactly what we're walking into.
<</dialog>>

She was right. She was always right about risk   the professional assessment of a woman whose survival depended on accurate threat calculation. The route was the route. The goblins were the goblins. The leg was the leg. None of these variables were negotiable. What was negotiable was how we prepared for the intersection of all three.

<<dialog "Mira">>
There's more. The surveyor   Marsh. Voss told me about him. He has conditions.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Conditions.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Consortium assessors aren't soldiers. They're academics with expense accounts and a deeply inflated sense of their own importance. Marsh has requirements: no travel after dark, hot food at camp, his equipment carried separately from our gear, and   this is the one that made me want to hit Voss   he insists on a daily written progress report filed at each waypoint.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
A daily written progress report. On an escort job. Through goblin territory.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
"The Consortium requires documentation of all contracted activities," Voss said. With a straight face. While signing a contract that's sending two half-broke mercenaries and a man who's afraid of dirt into a forest full of things that eat people.
<</dialog>>

