We ate dinner in the common room. Stew again   the Broken Antler's culinary repertoire was not what you'd call extensive. The innkeeper had exactly three modes: stew, bread-and-cheese, and "we're closed." Tonight was stew night, which meant gray meat in brown liquid with root vegetables that had been boiled until they'd lost any ambition they might have once held.

The common room was busy. Rest-day eve   the informal holiday that dock workers and laborers had invented for themselves, the night before their one day off, when they came out in force to spend tomorrow's wages on tonight's ale and pretend that the coming week didn't exist yet. The noise was welcome after the quiet tension of the afternoon. The hearth fire was blazing. A bard in the corner was playing a lute with more enthusiasm than skill, producing a sound that was technically music the way the Antler's ale was technically a beverage   it met the minimum requirements of the definition without actually satisfying anyone.

We took our corner booth. The usual position. Backs to the wall. Eyes on the door. The comfortable paranoia of two people who'd spent too long expecting trouble to pretend they weren't watching for it.

